  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.2. Increased capacity of member States to ensure safe and healthy working conditions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. In April 2020, the Government of the Republic of Namibia has adopted and issued Labour Directives relating to COVID-19: State of Emergency - COVID-19 Regulations. The regulations deal with the expansion of negotiation topics during a dismissal; Health and Safety measures to prevent the spread of COVID-19; rights to sickness and employee compensation benefits for employees who contract COVID-19 or suffers from a COVID-19 related post-traumatic stress disorder during the course of his or her employment.

2. In April 2021, the government further adopted the Namibia COVID-19/Occupational Safety and Health &quot;Keeping Namibia Safe at Work&quot; Guidelines 2021. The Guidelines aim to promote the integration of COVID-19 prevention and management into the workplace safety and health management and outlines minimum preventative measures that employers should take to curb the spread of COVID-19 in workplaces. The ILO provided technical support through webinars and training courses in which Namibian officials attended including the following: ARLAC Virtual Tripartite Workshop on Promoting Workplace Compliance with OSH Policies, Systems, Programmes and legal Frameworks from 14 to 18 September 2020; ARLAC Basic Labour Market Academy: Intake 6 (24 March and 12 May 2021), Intake 7 (14 April and 2 June 2021), and Intake 8 (28 June and 18 August 2021). In August 2020, Namibian officials were also part of the experts that developed the AUDA-NEPAD Guidelines on COVID19 and OSH for domestication by member states.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">7.2.1. Number of member States with national OSH policies or programmes, accompanied by institutional frameworks, addressing specific risks</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
